    I think it can be shown that all that is needed are propositions and propositional
    functions, du'u (which should probably be nu) and ka (propositions with holes
    in them).Â Events in Lojban are strange, because they all exist (or, atÂ
   least, are) but we seldom talk about their being realized or any of the usual
    abstraction talk.Â The other abstractors are even harder.Â They involve two
    factors: intensional contexts (or, at least, marking places where some normal
    rules don't apply) and indirect discourse (which-- like direct quotes --
   are intensional).Â Some of them are of rather limited familiarity: the sensual
    ones, say, which are not quite sense data nor even hallucinations, or the
    representational ones.Â The notion of a general abstraction is basically
   unintelligible and seems to be there for "completeness". Most abstractions
    abstract in a particular way (see the functions on worlds reading for some)
    and most intensional contexts are generated by predicates that allow such
    contexts (and occasionally require them). [...] 
 
